# GitHub Copilot Instructions — HOTL Project

This project follows Human-on-the-Loop (HOTL) development principles.

## Approach to suggestions

- Suggest minimal implementations that satisfy stated requirements (YAGNI)
- Prioritize test coverage alongside implementation
- Flag security-sensitive patterns (hardcoded secrets, unvalidated inputs, missing auth checks)

## Risk awareness

When suggesting changes to auth, encryption, billing, or access control:
- Add a comment noting this is high-risk code requiring human review
- Do not auto-complete sensitive logic — suggest structure and let human fill in details

## Code review awareness

When reviewing code or receiving review feedback:
- Verify each claim against the actual codebase before acting
- Flag findings with file:line references when possible
- BLOCK-level issues take priority over WARN and NOTE

## Verification mindset

Always suggest test code alongside implementation. Prefer testable patterns.

## Design-doc structure rules

Design-doc structure rules live in the canonical brainstorming skill (Claude Code: `skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md`; Cline: `cline/rules/hotl-brainstorming.md`). Feature/phase design docs use the seven required sections (Intent / Verification / Governance contracts plus Scope / Decisions / Surface / Risks & Open Questions); `document-lint.sh` warns on missing sections and on implementation leakage (file:line refs, code blocks > 10 lines, dense flag lines). Initiative / architecture / contract / reference docs are exempt this pass. Doctrine: design says shape; the workflow file and the code say exact bytes. Lint runs as warnings only — never block on lint findings; the human decides if a finding is blocking.
